60
00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.810
Fast fact number one, fan question from Maddie Jensen.

61
00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:13.850
I noticed that Ken Kwapis directed the finale for season three along with the finale of season two.

62
00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:18.290
Both episodes are crucial to the Jim-Pam plotline of the show.

63
00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:25.290
Was this an intentional decision to have the same director in charge of these important episodes or was it just a happy accident?

64
00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.230
Well, this was very intentional.

65
00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:30.990
I'm so glad you said that because I was sitting here and I wasn't sure.

66
00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.450
I was like, what is she going to say?

67
00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:40.890
And I was like, I hope it was thought out because Ken was so caring about the different dynamics and relationships on the show.

68
00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:42.270
He had been with us since the pilot.

69
00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.730
We had such a trust for his vision.

70
00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:49.650
And if you were about to say, it was just a scheduling thing, I was going to be like, no.

71
00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:59.530
No, I traded some emails with Ken Zabornak, and he told me that he and Greg always wanted Ken Kwapis to direct milestone episodes.

72
00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:08.350
So season openers, season closers, or any episodes with big story movement, especially if they involve Jim and Pam.

73
00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:11.450
He wasn't always available, but that was the intention.

74
00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:24.470
And if you look back, Ange, you'll see that oftentimes it was Ken Kwapis, Greg Daniels, Paul Feig, or Paul Lieberstein who would direct these big milestone episodes,

75
00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:31.370
which makes sense because those guys were kind of the original team of visionaries for the show.

76
00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.930
And they were with us since the first season.

77
00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:38.270
Yeah, so they knew every character's journey, and I think that was super important.

78
00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.510
That was sometimes a learning curve for new directors.

79
00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:49.690
If they hadn't seen every single episode, they would come in and maybe not understand why a character was making a certain choice.

80
00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.210
And I remember the cast would sometimes just bring them up to speed.

81
00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:54.350
Oh, I did.

82
00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:56.670
I remember I was in the scene.

83
00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:01.770
I forget who the director was, but they were like, so let's have Angela over there, like, cutting the meat or something.

84
00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.490
It was like a party episode.

85
00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.250
And I was like, oh, she wouldn't touch that meat.

86
00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:06.070
She's a vegetarian.

87
00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:07.770
She wouldn't go near it.

88
00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:08.810
Yes.

89
00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:12.510
And they're like, oh, okay, I guess I'll put you over there by the bag of chips.

90
00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:13.610
I'm like, yes, that's better.

91
00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:15.270
Exactly.

92
00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:23.710
So this created a really nice sense of consistency for those big episodes to bring people back who knew us all so well.

93
00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:24.530
All right.

94
00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:26.530
Fast fact number two.

95
00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:35.250
This is a fan question from Beatrix Smith, Brooke B., Amelia C., Erica P., and Supa Nanduri.

96
00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:46.190
Did the conversation at the beach between Jim and Pam that we see throughout this episode, did it have to be recreated like the kiss on casino night?

97
00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:50.350
Or was it a deleted scene that was added later without audio?

98
00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:51.510
What's the story?

99
00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:56.250
Well, guys, this was a new scene.

100
00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:00.610
But we knew about it when we were shooting beach games.

101
00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:04.670
So we shot this during the beach games shoot.

102
00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:07.530
We did not have to go back to the dam.

103
00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.070
Oh, yeah.

104
00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:09.430
We couldn't get that location again.

105
00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.110
So they shot it while we were there.

106
00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:14.910
And, Jenna, I remember that they brought Ken Quapis to direct that.

107
00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:16.950
And that was actually very common.

108
00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:19.510
That wasn't like anything weird to Harold Ramis.

109
00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:25.910
That was really common on a show if you had a cross-board moment where you had a scene that was in another episode,

110
00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:31.810
the director of that episode would come and direct that to sort of complete their story.

111
00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:40.950
So Ken came back and, Jenna, I read that this was supposed to be in the beginning of the show, like the cold open.

112
00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:49.290
Yes, just like that jump from casino night to gay witch hunt, we were going to originally open this episode.

113
00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:54.950
The cold open was going to be watching this scene between Jim and Pam on the beach.

114
00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:56.790
I'm so glad it wasn't that way.

115
00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.670
I think it creates so much more tension.

116
00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:01.550
Yeah.

117
00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:05.150
To leave it until later as a flashback.

118
00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:13.810
And, you know, Jenna, it really did add to the tension because now we know as the audience that they had had this moment.

119
00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:21.290
And so when Pam is saying goodbye and good luck and all of that, it's layered with this moment that they shared at Beach Games.

120
00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:23.650
And I love that our show did this.

121
00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.030
Our show didn't always just spoon feed the audience.

122
00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:28.930
It just had them like sort of pick up in the middle.

123
00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:30.390
And I just loved it.

124
00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:38.150
It was during the editing process that they decided to take it out of the cold open and put it later.

125
00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:45.450
Dave Rogers said in the DVD commentary that it was Mike Schur's idea to make it a flashback later in the episode.

126
00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:46.830
I love that.

127
00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:47.230
I know.

128
00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:47.910
He's so smart.

129
00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:49.090
He's such a smarty.

